#: src/macros/hygiene.md:6
msgid "Hygiene"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/hygiene.md:8
msgid ""
"A subtle aspect of macro systems is their degree of **hygiene**, or "
"independence from the lexical environment of their expansion."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/hygiene.md:11
msgid ""
"Macro hygiene enables macros to avoid accidentally being influenced by or "
"polluting the scope of the code surrounding their call sites."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/hygiene.md:14
msgid "In this section, we will cover:"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/hygiene.md:16
msgid "What macro hygiene is and why it is important."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/hygiene.md:17
msgid "How unhygienic macros can result in bugs or impede understanding code."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/hygiene.md:18
msgid ""
"The extent to which Rust macros are hygienic and the how partial hygiene in "
"Rust macros works."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/hygiene.md:24
msgid ""
"Explain that in many preprocessor-based languages (such as C), macros are "
"completely unhygienic, operating solely on raw tokens and potentially "
"interacting with the lexical environment differently at each expansion. This "
"means that the ability to reason about macros agnostic of the context in "
"which they will expand is extremely limited. This can lead to bugs when "
"names used in macros coincide with names used at their call sites."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/hygiene.md:30
msgid ""
"The notion of hygiene may be familiar to students who know LISP, as it "
"famously exhibits fully hygienic macros."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/hygiene.md:32
msgid ""
"This slide serves as a transition into the detailed discussion of macro "
"hygiene."
msgstr ""
